====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|en|/ˈlɪv/|[ˈlɪv]}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-live-verb.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪv|s=1}}
* {{hyph|en|live}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be [[alive]]; to have [[life]].
#: {{ux|en|He's not expected to '''live''' for more than a few months.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To have [[permanent]] [[residence]] somewhere, to [[inhabit]], to [[reside]].
#: {{syn|en|range<qq:fauna>}}
#: {{ux|en|I '''live''' at 2a Acacia Avenue.&emsp; At that time I'd been '''living''' in a camper for about six months.}}
#* {{RQ:Besant Ivory Gate|chapter=Prologue|page=16|passage=Athelstan Arundel walked home all the way, foaming and raging. No omnibus, cab, or conveyance ever built could contain a young man in such a rage. His mother '''lived''' at Pembridge Square, which is four good measured miles from Lincoln's Inn.}}
#* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=10|passage=It was a joy to snatch some brief respite, and find himself in the rectory drawing–room. Listening here was as pleasant as talking; just to watch was pleasant. The young priests who '''lived''' here wore cassocks and birettas; their faces were fine and mild, yet really strong, like the rector's face; and in their intercourse with him and his wife they seemed to be brothers.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive|informal}} (of an object) to have its proper place; to normally be stored.
##: {{ux|en|I washed your gravy boat. Where does it '''live'''?}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[survive]]; to [[persevere]]; to [[continue]].
#* {{RQ:Tennyson In Memoriam|XXXI|50|When Lazarus left his charnel-cave,<br>⁠And home to Mary’s house return’d, {{...}}<br>‘Where wert thou, brother, those four days?’<br>⁠There '''lives''' no record of reply,<br>⁠Which telling what it is to die<br>Had surely added praise to praise.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[endure]] in [[memory]]; to [[escape]] [[oblivion]].
#: {{ux|en|Her memory '''lives''' in that song.}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|date=March 14, 2017|author=Stuart James| title=Leicester stun Sevilla to reach last eight after Kasper Schmeichel save| url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2017/mar/14/leicester-city-sevilla-champions-league-match-report| passage=He has now overseen three straight victories since taking over from Claudio Ranieri and this latest win, against one of the best teams in Europe, will '''live''' long in the memory for every Leicester supporter.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20261013072902/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2017/mar/14/leicester-city-sevilla-champions-league-match-report|archivedate=13 October 2026}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|hyperbole}} To [[cope]].
#: {{ux|en|You'll just have to '''live''' with it!&emsp; I can't '''live''' in a world without you.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To pass life in a specified manner.
#: {{ux|en|It is difficult to '''live''' in poverty. &emsp; And they '''lived''' happily ever after.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To spend, as one's life; to pass; to maintain; to continue in, constantly or habitually.
#: {{ux|en|To '''live''' an idle or a useful life.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|passage=Many people write their romances, others '''live''' them; Honore de Balzac did both.| title=[[S:Women in the Life of Balzac/Conclusion|Women in the Life of Balzac]]| author=Juanita Helm Floyd| year=1921}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|date=December 19, 2011|author=Kerry Brown|title= Kim Jong-il obituary|url= http://www.guardian.co.uk/world/2011/dec/19/kim-jong-il| passage=By 1980, South Korea had overtaken its northern neighbour, and was well on its way to being one of the Asian tigers – high-performing economies, with democratic movements ultimately winning power in the 1990s. The withdrawal of most Soviet aid in 1991, with the fall of the Soviet empire, pushed North Korea further down. Kim Il-sung had held a genuine place on North Korean people's affections. His son was regarded as a shadowy playboy, with rumours circulating over the years that he imported Russian and Chinese prostitutes, and '''lived''' a life of profligacy and excess.}}
#* {{RQ:Economist|title=[http://www.economist.com/news/leaders/21578665-nearly-1-billion-people-have-been-taken-out-extreme-poverty-20-years-world-should-aim Towards the end of poverty]
|date=2013-06-01|volume=407|issue=8838|page=11
|passage=But poverty’s scourge is fiercest below $1.25 (the average of the 15 poorest countries’ own poverty lines, measured in 2005 dollars and adjusted for differences in purchasing power): people below that level [[live]] lives that are poor, nasty, brutish and short.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To act [[habitually]] in [[conformity]] with; to [[practice]]; to [[exemplify]] in one's [[way of life]].
#* {{RQ:Foxe Martyrs|passage=to '''live''' the Gospel}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=Body Politic|year=1971|number=1|page=8|author=Bart Moncq|text=The key to our freedom then, isn't begging for tolerance, but '''living''' our full rights.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|passage=Change happens from the inside out and this great resource can show you how to '''live''' the habits that build personal and professional effectiveness.|isbn=0978833406|title=Motivation at Work|year=2006|author=Laura Cardone|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=BiBfY37WbHEC}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|author=Jon Henley|title=The aggressive, outrageous, infuriating (and ingenious) rise of BrewDog|date=2016-03-24|url=https://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/2016/mar/24/the-aggressive-outrageous-infuriating-and-ingenious-rise-of-brewdog|passage=In short, he argues, in the modern era, “The only way to build a brand is to '''live''' that brand. You have to '''live''' the values and the mission, then let the customer decide.”|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20261015140454/https://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/2016/mar/24/the-aggressive-outrageous-infuriating-and-ingenious-rise-of-brewdog|archivedate=15 October 2026}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To live as; to live being.
#* {{RQ:Marlowe Tamburlaine|part=1|scene=ii|page=23|passage=at leaſt admit vs libertie,<br>Euen as thou hopſt to be eternized,<br>By '''liuing''' Aſias mightie Emperour.}}
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To outlast danger; {{lb|en|of a|ship or boat}} to [[float]].
#: {{ux|en|That rockslide trapped me in a cave, and I was trapped for three days, but I '''lived'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|No ship could '''live''' in such a storm.}}
#: {{ux|en|{{q|in a video game}} I don't know how I '''lived''' that, I should've died like 8 times.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Twelfth Night|act=I|scene=ii|passage=a strong mast that '''lived''' upon the sea}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|followed by {{m|en|on}}, {{m|en|upon}}, or {{m|en|by}}}} To [[maintain]] or [[support]] one's existence; to [[provide]] for oneself; to [[feed]]; to [[subsist]].
#: {{ux|en|It is hard to '''live''' on the minimum wage. &emsp; They '''lived''' on stale bread. &emsp; Man shall not '''live''' by bread alone.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|informal}} To [[make the most of]] life; to experience a [[full]], [[rich]] [[life]].
#: {{ux|en|I'm sick of spending every day studying at home: I want to go out there and '''live'''!}}
# {{lb|en|figurative|intransitive}} To spend a great deal of time in a particular place.
#: {{ux|en|She '''lives''' on the Internet.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* Throughout Late Middle English and Early Modern English in Midlands and Northern dialects, the present participle form '''livand''' co-occurs with the form '''living'''.

=====Synonyms=====
* {{sense|to have permanent residence somewhere}} {{l|en|dwell}}; See also [[Thesaurus:reside]]
* {{sense|to survive}} {{l|en|go on}}, {{l|en|last}}, {{l|en|remain}}; See also [[Thesaurus:persist]]

